WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.530
Narrator: In 2016,
Toronto spent $31 million

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:08.600
to fend off a raccoon invasion.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.260
The masked critters were everywhere,

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:13.900
pooping on porches, stopping traffic,

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.990
and infesting attics, and they're not

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:19.000
just taking over Toronto.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.490
Reports of raccoon vandalism
have plagued cities

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:25.560
like Portland, Chicago, and New York City,

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.070
and as raccoon-ridden cities know,

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:29.883
we can't seem to stop them.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:36.380
Between the 1930s and 1980s,
the US raccoon population

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:40.540
increased twentyfold, and
it's still going strong.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:43.810
From 2014 to 2015, raccoon complaints

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:46.320
in Brooklyn nearly doubled,

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:51.320
so how are these masked bandits
making it in big cities?

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.920
Well, for starters, they can
digest just about anything

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.490
from fish and acorns in the forest

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:01.340
to dog food and pizza on the street,

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:05.750
and just like humans, raccoons
usually prefer the pizza,

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:07.960
which is why they flock from woodland

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.070
to city in the first place.

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:13.630
In Brooklyn, for example,
captured raccoons sometimes

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:17.370
get relocated to Prospect
Park and nearby forests,

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:20.850
but wildlife biologists report
they often head right back

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:23.330
to the dumpster-packed city streets.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:26.100
It's just about impossible to stop them,

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:29.350
as Toronto discovered
after it spend millions

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:32.600
on raccoon-proof waste bins.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:36.950
Unlike traditional bins, the
lids had special gravity locks,

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:39.330
which open when a garbage truck arm

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:41.510
turns the bin upside down.

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:45.370
The idea was that if you cut
off their major food source,

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:49.000
they would skip town,
but that didn't happen.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.910
In fact, one year later, a
wildlife-control business

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:56.480
reported that raccoon-related
work had doubled.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:00.210
Finally, a clever raccoon
was caught on camera

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:02.300
jailbreaking the new bin.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.630
How did she outwit an entire city?

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:09.420
Well, study after study
has revealed that raccoons

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:12.900
are considerably smarter than
your average medium-sized

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:17.660
critter. Turns out raccoon
brains have more neurons packed

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:21.470
into their brains than other
animals of the same size.

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:25.310
In fact, they have the same
neuron density as primates,

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:29.080
who are notoriously smart,
and their clever brains help

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:32.700
explain why raccoons
can open complex locks,

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.340
solve puzzles with ease, and
even come up with solutions

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:39.470
to problems that
scientists didn't think of.

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:42.610
Add to that their ultrasensitive hands,

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:47.210
er, paws, which have four
times as many sensory receptors

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:51.130
as their feet. This helps
them to feel subtle textures

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:53.660
like special trashcan lids in Toronto

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:57.080
and even open locks without looking.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:58.930
And unfortunately for us,

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:02.020
driving them away is a fool's errand.

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:04.550
Studies show that after mass removal,

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.370
populations tend to rebound

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:08.970
to their previous levels in a year.

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:11.840
After all, females can start giving birth

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:16.200
at just 1 year old and can
have as many as eight kits

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:17.840
in a single year.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:22.080
This quick breeding is also
why experts say mass cullings

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.160
aren't a long-term solution.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:26.500
And while they're awfully cute,

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:29.460
the damage they can cause is not.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:31.760
When raccoons nest in buildings,

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:33.820
they can destroy insulation,

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:37.890
chew up wires, and tear
holes through walls,

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:41.110
and it can cost you
hundreds or even thousands

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:43.580
of dollars to repair that kind of damage.

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:45.470
One raccoon was even caught

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:49.800
destroying over $3,000 worth of artwork,

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:54.640
and just removing them can
cost $300 to $500 a pop.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:58.410
Plus, the poop they leave
behind can contain roundworms

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:00.950
and other parasites,
which can enter your lungs

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:05.280
when you breathe or get tracked
into your home by your pets.

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:08.480
Even worse, raccoons can transmit diseases

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:13.140
like canine distemper and,
in rare cases, rabies.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:15.900
So it's understandable
that cities are trying

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.800
to find some way, any way, to manage them.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.250
Can't do a thing about
it, just chase them off.

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:24.440
They come back.

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:27.310
Narrator: If nothing else,
it's a lesson learned.

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:29.150
We may have built the cities,

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:31.593
but we don't necessarily rule them.

